WEBVTT

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.830
Ain't you 'bout due for a good retradin'?

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:44.170
Near as I can recollect, you're 'bout more down to the fabric.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.090
Admiral will check 'em for you.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.850
I can't now long, I'm busy.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.230
What I'm interested in are groceries.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.210
Oh, yeah.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.580
Five pounds of sugar.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.580
Can of Harmony.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.000
A box of crackers.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.070
- Morning, Lam.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:02.070
- Hey, hi, Earl.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.070
- Morning, Mr. Pinellar.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.070
- Morning, Earl.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.370
- Morning, Abner.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:06.200
- Morning, Abner.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.380
- Boss says you wanted to send me about the draft.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:10.590
- Oh, yeah.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:15.460
Want to get you to fill out one of these occupation cards here

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:16.460
so that I can--

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:19.460
- I ain't had no occupation, no.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:20.640
- That's right.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.640
As far as I know, you ain't never done a lick of work

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:23.640
in your whole life.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:26.820
Still, I sorta hate to put loafer down there.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.600
Feared you might wind up in the bakery.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.140
You mean I won't have to go in the army then?

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.970
No, sir, I want you to get out here and get yourself a job

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.350
so as I can draft you right out of it.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:39.920
Okay, Abner.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.220
And another thing, don't stick your hand in that apple barrel on the way out, neither.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:45.600
Don't need to.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.000
Got one on the way in.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.440
And it'll farm it.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:50.740
Let's see, that's 60.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:51.900
You never take the tomatoes.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:52.740
That's 10 off.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:53.840
That's down to 50 off.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:54.740
From all days off.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:55.580
I know that's right.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:56.580
And you add the nine.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:57.600
That makes 59.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.440
First one is...

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.440
69 and 10 is 79 points.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.890
I hope you don't mind it, the torn out, but my young'un got a hold of my rationing book.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:10.510
Well, you have to take that up with the rationing board, then.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.180
Uh-oh, here comes trouble.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.680
Oh, how do you do, Abner?

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.240
Well, I can tell from a tone of your voice there's something wrong. What's the trouble?

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.560
Well, my little boy tore these stamps out of my rationing book.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:26.750
Here's like I've heard that before.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.000
He does it every time I back turn, I just don't know what I'm doing.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.070
going to do with that boy?

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.200
Well, I know what I'd do if he'd turn his back to me.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.950
But if I was you, I'd believe I'd make a magician out of him.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:38.850
A magician?

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:44.490
Yeah, I've been keeping track here, and according to my figures, he's tore nine more stamps

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.590
out of the book this month than you had to start with.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:47.310
No.

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.270
Er, no, no, my mistake.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:49.710
Yes?

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:50.570
Ten.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.930
All right, Granny, you've done good, haven't you?

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.000
Be quiet, Mom, be quiet.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:01.860
- Good day, ladies and gentlemen.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.060
First, let me-- - Who's that?

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.360
- This is Chester W. Marshall,

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.940
head of the CAITWE. - Yeah, he told you.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.790
- Today, my message is to the common man.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.180
It is more than a message.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.900
It is an appeal for you, the common man,

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.700
to cooperate with us in this vital search

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.550
for new ideas and inventions.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.190
- Inventions, do you hear that?

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:22.090
- Yeah, I hear it.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:23.250
- Today, our appeal is to you.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.030
As you know, many of our greatest discoveries

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.330
were not the result of long, strenuous laboratory work

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.590
and research, but were stumbled on by accident.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.780
Badogies, I've bound you. I can invent something that'll make Pine Ridge famous.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.740
If you do, it'll be an accident. I'll guarantee you that.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.820
Something was done by men like you, and you and you.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:43.260
Men from Vermont, Utah, Maine, Kentucky, California.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:44.820
He left out Arkansas.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:46.100
Why, of course he did.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.760
Let's dig into history and notable discoveries that were made.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:50.980
Take Isaac Newton.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.220
Mom, you've heard of Isaac Newton.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:54.860
I've heard of Big Newton.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:56.170
This is Isaac.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.230
One day he was sitting out under an apple tree,

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:00.000
an hour.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.160
fell down and accidental hit him on top the head. Accidental hit him, mind you. And after

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:10.620
that he had gravities. And a knot on his head. Yeah. And then there was Benjamin Franklin.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.720
Oh, Amber, quit listening to that priddle-craddle and pish posh. First thing you know, you'll

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:22.580
be starting out on another one of your inventing sprees. Good morning, love. Morning, Aunt

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:30.000
Charity. Oh, yes, you called up about getting some extra gasoline. Oh, doggies, aren't

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.140
I'm going back here where I won't be interrupted.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.040
I'm sorry, Aunt Charity, but this ain't a black market.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.960
I'm feared I can't authorize no extra gas left, and it's for essential use.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:41.600
But, Mom, this is essential.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.420
Not driving clean out to your daughter's place, ain't.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.930
But, Mom, I just became a new grandmother.

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:49.750
You did? Well, good for you.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.170
Six pounds, eight ounces. A boy!

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.420
Oh, well, that's essential.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.110
Grannies, we ought to be able to figure something out of that.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:00.000
You can't raise some babies, you know.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:00.220
No.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:02.240
They never have before, Lum.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:04.980
Wait a minute, I've got an idea.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:11.490
Yeah, I just happened to think I need a new fire watcher out near your daughter's place.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.250
That'll give you just enough gasoline to get to your post.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:15.740
Thank you, Lum.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:18.780
And I want you to report for duty tonight with full equipment.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.100
A pocket full of safety pins.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:23.860
Oh, just a minute.

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:25.850
Here, take off your hat.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:28.930
This ought to do it.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:30.640
there

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.840
here's your old hat

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.620
or I mean your other hat

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:38.700
oh thank you

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:44.140
without the many conveniences invented by the common man

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:45.100
would not be worth living

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:48.600
ideas are valuable so don't let them get away from you

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.260
and I say to you the common man

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:52.280
no matter what you have little or big

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:53.180
work on it

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:54.780
are you still listening to that thing?

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.020
be quiet now

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:00.000
you will find that I am the one man in Washington who is easy to

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.100
This is Chester W. Marshall of the CAITWE, signing off.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.960
Well, I got a lot of inventing to do, Lon.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.260
If you want me, I'll be back here.

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:13.260
Well, don't blow up face like you done once before.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.580
Abner, come on, home. It's two hours past closing time now.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.420
I can't leave till this stuff gets done cooking.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:38.260
What is that junk you're mixing up?

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:41.400
Oh, nothing. Just a pot of dynamite.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:42.280
Dynamite?

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:45.480
Don't join me that way, long. This stuff is liable to explode.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:48.360
It's so dangerous. I wish now I hadn't even mixed it up.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:49.620
Well, throw it out.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:53.640
Throw it out? I wouldn't even carry that stuff out, long.

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.420
Well, how do you aim to get shut of it?

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:57.660
I'm just hoping it'll boil away.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:00.000
Now look, Abner, you know I think the world

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.060
and all of you. Love to encourage you in whatever you're doing, but you're just carrying this

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:11.590
in, Vincent, too far. Oh, sassy-fass. Sassy-fass. Well, yeah, you're going to bankrupt us, bringing

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:17.330
stuff out of the store back here and mixing it up and wasting it. Don't do that! Now, this

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:24.130
stuff here, for instance, what is that? Oh, that's just some, uh, synophetic licorice I

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:30.000
made up, but it never turned out very good, Lonnie. You can't chew it. It's too stretchy. I see there. Brung it

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.220
out of the store and just ruined it.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.260
I never brung it out of the store.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:38.460
I made that out of sweet gum and stretch berries and tar and stuff.

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:40.230
All right, Granny's, wait a minute, Abner.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:42.090
I believe we've got something here.

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:42.710
Huh?

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:45.580
The bee-body?

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:47.080
Oh, come in, Gomer.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.580
You have some more of that stuff you call licorice?

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:52.510
You mean that you could actually eat that stuff?

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:54.670
No, I need a slingshot side of it.

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:57.890
This one here shoots a marble farther than any one I ever had.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:00.000
Oh, let's see that thing.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:00.920
Sonny?

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:06.920
Grand Sappy Spears made some sleeve garters out of it, and Cedric, he made some suspenders.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.070
Everything I make turns out wrong.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:12.070
Sleeve garters and galaxies, huh?

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:13.000
Yeah.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:14.020
Shoot it.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.020
Oh, ain't got nothing to shoot with.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:16.020
Here's a rock.

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:16.990
Shoot it.

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:18.990
I don't know much about working these things.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:19.990
How do you do it?

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:21.990
Just pull it back and let it go.

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:22.990
Like that, huh?

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:27.650
Yeah.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:30.000
What's all the shooting for?

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.180
What do I have this thing loaded with, anyway?

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:34.680
I don't know, but I'm going to get out of here.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:35.880
Hey, you hurt, Mom?

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:37.280
I don't think so, Abner.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:41.820
I cross my heart and promise I'll never try to invent nothing else as long as I live.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:42.820
Well, you don't have to.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:43.860
You've done invented it.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:45.360
That's the greatest thing I've ever seen.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:47.880
It was a rice mortar explosion, weren't it?

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.300
Oh, I ain't talking about that.

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:50.820
I'm talking about this stuff right here.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:53.620
We've got to get some of this to that Mr. Marshall in Washington.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:56.820
Mom, he ain't got time to sit around and eat licorice.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:58.180
This ain't licorice, you idiot.

00:09:58.180 --> 00:09:59.240
That's synathetic's rubber.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:09:59.960
Lookie here.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:02.240
No good, huh? No good in order to make

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.160
national heroes out of us. You mean

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.160
we can make money out of that? Well, it ain't

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:08.000
the money that counts, Abner. It's

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.950
being patriotic and working for the

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:11.780
government. That's the important thing

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:13.440
right now. Come on, we're going to Washington.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.400
Going to Washington? Why, sure.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.720
Abner, I believe I see a chance

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:19.560
to realize a lifelong ambition.

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:21.790
Be one of them dollar a year men.

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:23.710
Is that a steady salary?

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:25.560
Oh, for goodness sakes.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.080
Hello, got them down,

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:30.000
store. Mel Grinchpiers are doing the

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.170
talking? No, no, no, I'm the

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:37.700
provider here. Yeah, and no, I'm not

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:39.920
the actual proprietor, no, they're

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:41.720
going to Washington, and I'm going

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:43.200
to run the store for them while

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.850
they're gone. Yeah, that's right,

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.370
yeah. All right, oh, mommy did a

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:52.060
life check. Well, Lum, that's a nice

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:53.860
suit you got on there. You like it,

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:55.870
huh? I sure do. I never got no

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:57.290
cuffs on the britches, but I got a

00:10:57.290 --> 00:11:00.000
baseball man. Now, Grandpap, you sure you

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.320
We're going to be able to look after the store for us while we're going.

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:03.300
Why, sure.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:07.500
I'll run this pigeon-toed store like it ain't never been run before.

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:09.320
Yeah, that's what we're afraid of.

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:11.560
Are you sure you understand all about the point system?

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:13.080
Oh, yes, yes.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.180
If they want cheese and crackers, a point over there.

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:17.120
If they want dress goods, a point over there.

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:26.250
What's that?

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:30.000
Well, that's all the town folks are hearing you're going to Washington.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:32.770
and they come down to give you a big candle.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:34.090
- Well, good birthday.

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:36.890
- Come on, ma'am, we gotta get going.

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:58.860
Thank you, folks, thank you, thank you.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:00.000
It's mighty saughty,

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:00.440
you all

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:01.140
to come down here

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:01.520
and give us

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:02.360
that you send off.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:03.600
I'll tell you

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:04.360
the harp strings

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:05.120
of memory strike

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:05.880
a tender chord

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:06.520
as I look out

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:07.080
amongst all

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:07.980
these familiar faces.

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:09.880
You know,

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:10.600
Pine Ridge

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:11.340
has always been

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:12.220
a small town,

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:13.160
but I grant you

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:13.680
from now on

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:14.420
you watch her grow.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:18.200
It'll be known

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:18.920
fair and wide

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:19.780
as the birthplace

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:20.320
of them two

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:21.340
famous inventors,

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:22.060
me and Abner.

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:24.490
My tourists

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:25.070
will be flocking

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:25.990
in here from everywhere

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:27.910
to see where we lived

00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:28.530
and had

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.000
and done other history

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:37.230
Some day, when we get back from Washington, folks will more likely say, "Lum and Abner

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:38.230
slept here."

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:44.770
"Luke, you're going to have to enlarge your lunchroom, and Mose, you're going to have

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:49.730
to get another chair for your barbershop, all for goodness sakes."

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:59.730
"Abner, put down that sign and come on up here."

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:13.790
2015 we better get started it will wear the samples of my synesthetic robber right here. Oh, well, don't worry folks

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:16.530
When we get back from Washington, we'll have tar throw

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:22.870
And we ain't gonna let it go to our heads neither. We're coming home speaking to you same as always

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:58.130
that's come through the fine rails.

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:00.000
Well, this is Washington.

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:03.270
He's bigger than I thought he'd be long.

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:05.010
Well, this ain't all of it, silly.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:06.470
This is just the depot.

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:09.370
Oh, well, are we going to stay out at Mr. Marshall's house?

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.910
No, I don't think we better try to put up at their place,

